  "name": "Question 1",
  "problem": "Which of the following is a root of the polynomial ${v}^{4}-4{v}^{3}+7v-28?$\n\nI. $v=-4$\n\nII. $v=4$\n\nIII. $v=2$",
  "worked_solution": "- Substituting $-4$ for $v$ in the polynomial and evaluating, we get the following result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-4)^{4}-4(-4)^{3}+7(-4)-28 & = \\\\ 256+256-28-28 & = \\\\ 456\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe polynomial does *not* equal $0$ when $-4$ is substituted for $v.$ Therefore, $-4$ is *not*  a root.\n\n- Substituting $4$ for $v$ in the polynomial and evaluating, we get the following result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(4)^{4}-4(4)^{3}+7(4)-28 & = \\\\ 256-256+28-28 & = \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe polynomial is $0$ when $4$ is substituted for $v.$ Therefore, $4$ is a root.\n\n- Substituting $2$ for $v$ in the polynomial and evaluating, we get the following result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2)^{4}-4(2)^{3}+7(2)-28 & = \\\\ 16-32+14-28 & = \\\\ -30\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe polynomial does *not* equal $0$ when $2$ is substituted for $v.$ Therefore, $2$ is *not*  a root.\n\nTherefore, the root is $v=4,$ so the correct answer is \"II only\".",
